1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:04,525
A browser assertion cannot tell you when another service has processed a business

2
00:00:04,538 --> 00:00:09,625
event. Follow one transfer from the gateway to authoritative account state and then

3
00:00:09,638 --> 00:00:14,662
into a separate ledger projection. We will use the returned transfer identifier to

4
00:00:14,675 --> 00:00:18,900
connect those observations and apply a bounded wait only where the contract permits

5
00:00:18,912 --> 00:00:25,525
delay. The exercise runs across local processes. Its value is learning to distinguish

6
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:30,250
business commitment, asynchronous visibility, and diagnostic evidence without assuming

7
00:00:30,263 --> 00:00:33,125
that a quiet browser network means everything finished.

8
00:00:35,358 --> 00:00:40,520
Start the distributed laboratory and inspect its architecture. The gateway serves the

9
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:46,296
browser on port three thousand. Accounts holds balances, canonical transfers, and an

10
00:00:46,308 --> 00:00:51,496
event log on port three thousand one. Ledger runs separately on port three thousand

11
00:00:51,508 --> 00:00:56,496
two and polls that log to update its own projection. The startup coordinator checks

12
00:00:56,508 --> 00:01:02,220
all three services. A gateway health response alone describes the gateway process, so

13
00:01:02,233 --> 00:01:07,458
keep dependency readiness evidence separate. These boundaries let us reproduce timing

14
00:01:07,470 --> 00:01:11,046
differences without connecting to any external banking infrastructure.

15
00:01:13,256 --> 00:01:18,344
In this simulator, the debit, canonical transfer, and outgoing event are updated in

16
00:01:18,356 --> 00:01:23,831
one synchronous section of the accounts process. This illustrates an outbox: business

17
00:01:23,844 --> 00:01:28,794
state and the event describing it should not drift apart. However, the state is in

18
00:01:28,806 --> 00:01:34,119
memory. Restarting the process loses it, and there is no durable database or message

19
00:01:34,131 --> 00:01:40,519
broker. Describe the demonstrated guarantee precisely. A successful classroom run does

20
00:01:40,531 --> 00:01:45,294
not establish crash recovery, durable delivery, or transaction behavior across

21
00:01:45,306 --> 00:01:46,781
multiple production nodes.

22
00:01:48,994 --> 00:01:52,956
Submit the transfer once and capture the returned identifier from its successful

23
00:01:52,969 --> 00:01:58,432
response. Keep this identifier in the test's evidence object, together with the source

24
00:01:58,444 --> 00:02:03,307
account and expected amount. Do not search for the latest transfer across the whole

25
00:02:03,319 --> 00:02:08,544
system. Another test could create a newer record between your action and query.

26
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:14,232
Correlation should follow the value produced by this exact operation. The same

27
00:02:14,244 --> 00:02:19,657
identifier will later travel into the terminal adapter, allowing the web, API, and

28
00:02:19,669 --> 00:02:22,907
terminal observations to refer to one synthetic transfer.

29
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:29,052
First query the authoritative account and canonical transfer collection through the

30
00:02:29,065 --> 00:02:34,128
gateway. These endpoints preserve the original laboratory contract: after the

31
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:38,915
successful response, the balance is ninety thousand centavos and the transfer exists

32
00:02:38,928 --> 00:02:45,228
once. An incorrect authoritative balance is not an expected ledger delay. Fail that

33
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:50,428
assertion and investigate it directly. This ordering keeps the test honest about which

34
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:55,665
component owns each guarantee. It also prevents an overly generous polling loop from

35
00:02:55,678 --> 00:02:59,390
hiding a defect in a state transition that should already have completed.

36
00:03:01,622 --> 00:03:06,884
Now query the separate ledger endpoint, filtered by the source account. Its response

37
00:03:06,897 --> 00:03:10,710
contains the projected records and may also expose a projection cursor for

38
00:03:10,722 --> 00:03:16,760
diagnostics. Inspect the HTTP status before treating its body as business data.

39
00:03:17,622 --> 00:03:21,422
The endpoint is intentionally different from the canonical transfer collection,

40
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:28,097
despite similar records. Write both paths explicitly in the evidence matrix. If you

41
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:32,797
accidentally query the canonical endpoint twice, the test may look distributed while

42
00:03:32,810 --> 00:03:36,147
never observing whether the ledger process consumed the event at all.

43
00:03:38,368 --> 00:03:42,956
Use a bounded polling assertion to repeat the read until the exact transfer identifier

44
00:03:42,968 --> 00:03:49,143
appears. The polling body must not submit another transfer. It observes progress

45
00:03:49,156 --> 00:03:53,856
without creating new business effects. The excerpt checks membership within a

46
00:03:53,868 --> 00:03:58,380
five-second classroom deadline; afterward, inspect the matching record's amount,

47
00:03:58,543 --> 00:04:04,906
currency, source, and count. This deadline is a laboratory expectation, not a claim

48
00:04:04,918 --> 00:04:10,318
about production performance. On timeout, preserve the last response and correlation

49
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:14,630
identifier so the failure explains what was observed instead of reporting only that

50
00:04:14,643 --> 00:04:15,531
time elapsed.

51
00:04:17,754 --> 00:04:23,329
Compare polling with a fixed sleep. A fixed pause always waits the full duration, yet

52
00:04:23,342 --> 00:04:28,479
still fails if publication takes slightly longer. Polling can finish as soon as its

53
00:04:28,492 --> 00:04:32,754
business condition holds and produces a defined failure when the deadline expires.

54
00:04:33,617 --> 00:04:38,104
Browser locator waiting solves a different problem: it observes browser state, not the

55
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:43,417
ledger process. Choose the wait according to the state you are observing. If

56
00:04:43,429 --> 00:04:48,642
publication is consistently slow, inspect service behavior and the expectation before

57
00:04:48,654 --> 00:04:50,816
increasing the timeout to hide failures.

58
00:04:53,036 --> 00:04:57,274
Once the projected transfer appears, inspect the filtered collection rather than

59
00:04:57,286 --> 00:05:03,036
stopping at membership. Verify its amount, currency, source account, and completed

60
00:05:03,048 --> 00:05:08,011
status, and ensure the expected single record count holds for this isolated fixture.

61
00:05:08,874 --> 00:05:13,661
In the replay case, correlate both responses to the same canonical identifier and

62
00:05:13,674 --> 00:05:18,574
inspect the projection for one business record. This demonstrates the simulator's

63
00:05:18,586 --> 00:05:23,636
observed behavior for that scenario. It does not prove every message-delivery failure

64
00:05:23,648 --> 00:05:27,111
mode, because the local event mechanism is deliberately limited.

65
00:05:29,326 --> 00:05:33,489
When the projection deadline expires, build a short timeline from the available

66
00:05:33,501 --> 00:05:39,014
evidence. Record the transfer response, authoritative balance, canonical record,

67
00:05:39,189 --> 00:05:44,588
ledger response, and process health observations with the same reference. Then form a

68
00:05:44,601 --> 00:05:49,326
hypothesis: the transfer may have committed while publication or consumption failed.

69
00:05:50,189 --> 00:05:54,326
Do not label that hypothesis as a confirmed root cause until logs or another

70
00:05:54,338 --> 00:05:59,951
observation support it. Use evidence to locate the broken boundary before asking AI to

71
00:05:59,964 --> 00:06:01,638
explain a distributed timeout.

72
00:06:03,864 --> 00:06:09,539
Attach a compact correlation summary to the test result. Include the case, fixture,

73
00:06:09,702 --> 00:06:14,252
transfer reference, expected amount, and paths used for authoritative and projected

74
00:06:14,264 --> 00:06:20,514
observations. Exclude credentials and unrelated account data. A reviewer should be

75
00:06:20,527 --> 00:06:24,402
able to move from a failed assertion to the matching service evidence without guessing

76
00:06:24,414 --> 00:06:29,789
which request mattered. The displayed structure is an example report shape, not proof

77
00:06:29,802 --> 00:06:35,514
of an executed result. Populate it with values captured during the actual attempt, and

78
00:06:35,527 --> 00:06:38,626
link the corresponding artifacts after the run completes.

79
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:45,500
Your distributed checkpoint should answer four questions. Did the account change

80
00:06:45,513 --> 00:06:51,888
correctly? Did the canonical transfer exist exactly once? Did that same identifier

81
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:56,425
reach the separate projection within the classroom deadline? What evidence supports

82
00:06:56,438 --> 00:07:02,350
each answer? Keep any timeout or missing observation visible in the report. You now

83
00:07:02,362 --> 00:07:06,238
have a regression that crosses process boundaries while distinguishing commitment from

84
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:11,838
delayed visibility. Next, a browser mock will help compare focused UI evidence with an

85
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:12,975
integrated workflow.
